<Base title="How CalcsDesk will show its working | Methodology" description="The CalcsDesk standard for source references, visible assumptions, reproducible calculations and tested official examples." breadcrumb="Methodology">
  <article class="reading-page"><p class="eyebrow">The working, not just the answer</p><h1>Every number should<br /> have an explanation.</h1><p class="lead">CalcsDesk is being built around a simple standard: you should be able to see what a calculation uses and how it reaches a result.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Development status</strong><p>The first wage-only federal income-tax tool is available as a beta, with sourced 2026 rate data and automated schedule checks. Human review and the full calculator release checklist remain pending. The UK monthly-salary, limited UAE gratuity and India salary-regime calculators are also betas, with their own source and review notes. Other tools remain in preparation.</p></div><h2>Start with the right context.</h2><p>Choose the country and relevant region, then the tool. The finished calculation will state the tax year or effective period, currency, pay frequency and any eligibility assumptions.</p><h2>Use traceable rates.</h2><p>Before a tool is released, each changeable rate must have an official source URL and a verification date. The calculation page will link to the rate table it uses. A source-directory link by itself does not mean a rate has been verified.</p><h2>Test the working.</h2><p>Each calculator must reproduce at least two official worked examples before full release. The federal beta currently has official tax-schedule cross-checks and tests of the wage portions of IRS Medicare examples; those do not replace the full worked-example gate. Its tests must also cover relevant thresholds, rounding, invalid inputs and important edge cases.</p><h2>Explain the limits.</h2><p>The result will include the formula, a breakdown, inputs, assumptions and exclusions. Estimates are not tax, legal or financial advice, and may differ from a payroll system or an individual filing outcome.</p><h2>Keep the calculation local.</h2><p>The intended calculator flow runs in the browser without sending your entered figures to a server. These development pages do not send financial inputs to a server. The theme preference and an optional remembered country filter are saved on this device when browser storage is available. Wage and service inputs are not saved automatically.</p><h2>Check before relying on a result.</h2><p>A limited interactive beta stays noindex while its remaining example and human-review gates are pending. A tool still in preparation does not calculate an estimate. Verification labels and reviewer credentials will be shown only when the corresponding work has actually happened.</p><h2>How to reproduce a result</h2>
<p>Start by recording the calculator name, period and input labels. Keep annual income separate from a monthly payment. Write down the filing status, nation, regime or service assumptions you selected. When you compare estimates from two tools, use the same inputs and check that both outputs refer to the same period and the same set of charges.</p>
<p>Next, follow each line of the breakdown in order. For a bracket calculation, start from the displayed taxable amount and check the part assigned to each band. Compare the sum with the displayed tax before adding separate charges or applying adjustments. If the tool shows a monthly average, compare it with the annual estimate divided across the displayed number of months. Record any rounding difference at the step where it appears.</p>
<h2>What to compare for each beta</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="/us/tax/income-tax-calculator/">US federal tax</a> separates regular income tax from employee payroll taxes. Compare each line with the matching line in your reference before comparing totals. For a joint estimate, keep the two wage inputs separate.</li>
<li><a href="/uk/pay/salary-calculator/">UK monthly salary</a> combines an annual income tax estimate with monthly National Insurance. Check that the reference uses your selected nation and twelve equal monthly payments.</li>
<li><a href="/ae/gratuity/gratuity-calculator/">UAE gratuity</a> depends on basic wage and qualifying service days. Verify the service inputs and check the supported case before comparing a result with a settlement figure.</li>
<li><a href="/in/tax/income-tax-calculator/">India tax regimes</a> displays the taxable amount separately for each regime. Compare the deduction, rebate and final tax lines before drawing a conclusion from the difference between totals.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What to record when figures differ</h2>
<p>Record the official reference, its effective period and the first line where your calculation diverges. Use a fictional example that reproduces the discrepancy before sharing it. Check the <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a> for the current reporting process and the <a href="/updates/">update log</a> for changes to the calculator or its scope.</p>
